            "answer": "The 'Jotedar Thesis' (formulated by historians like Rajat and Ratnalekha Ray and synthesized by Sekhar Bandyopadhyay) challenges the traditional view that the Permanent Settlement created absolute, omnipotent Zamindars in Bengal. It demonstrates that in rural villages, the real de facto power was held by 'Jotedars'—a class of wealthy, resident rich peasants who owned large landholdings, controlled local agricultural credit, directly mobilized sharecroppers (Adhiars/Bargadars), and manipulated village markets. Because Zamindars were often urban absentee landlords, local Jotedars deliberately incited ryots to withhold rent payments, forcing Zamindars to default on revenue payments to the British, leading to the auctioning of zamindaris which the Jotedars themselves purchased.",
